# Copy to .env and edit what you need. Everything is optional.

# The owner key. Leave unset and one is generated on first start (printed in
# `docker compose logs cedar`, kept in the data volume as cedar.key).
# To pick your own (16+ characters of plain ASCII):  openssl rand -base64 24
# CEDAR_KEY=

# Shown in the app's Profile tab.
# CEDAR_SERVER_NAME=My Cedar server

# Host port the app connects to (default 8000).
# CEDAR_PORT=8000

# Which address the port is published on. 0.0.0.0 = every interface, so a
# phone on your Wi-Fi can connect directly. Set 127.0.0.1 when a reverse proxy
# on this same machine fronts the server: then nothing else can reach the
# plain-HTTP port (Docker publishes ports around the host firewall).
# CEDAR_BIND=0.0.0.0

# Reverse proxies whose X-Forwarded-For header the server trusts, so the
# wrong-key throttle sees real client addresses instead of the proxy's own.
# Comma-separated IPs or CIDRs; "*" only if the port is reachable through the
# proxy and nothing else.
# CEDAR_TRUSTED_PROXIES=127.0.0.1

# Size ceilings, in MB: file uploads, "import from link" downloads, and every
# other request body (pasted text, live pages).
# CEDAR_MAX_UPLOAD_MB=100
# CEDAR_MAX_FETCH_MB=60
# CEDAR_MAX_BODY_MB=8

# One uncached speech job at a time avoids competing inference on small CPUs.
# Raise for a GPU or multiple listeners if benchmarks support it.
# CEDAR_TTS_CONCURRENCY=1
# Generate up to three upcoming sentences into the cache while listening.
# Set 0 to disable speculative generation (for example on a busy shared server).
# CEDAR_TTS_LOOKAHEAD=3
# Shed least-recently-played sentence clips once the audio cache passes this
# size, in GiB. 0 keeps the cache unbounded (its historical behavior).
# CEDAR_AUDIO_CACHE_GB=4
# Kokoro CPU-time budget: 0 = unlimited; try 3 on a four-core N5105.
# The ARM64 profile defaults to 3, for the 6-core Snapdragon X2 Plus.
# CEDAR_KOKORO_CPUS=3

# Optional Pocket TTS override: cap its CPU use (default 2 cores).
# CEDAR_POCKET_CPUS=2
# Which engine the Pocket image contains: "python" (the PyTorch engine, works
# on any x86-64 or ARM64 host) or "rust" (a ~25 MB standalone binary, same
# /tts contract). The released x86-64 Rust build needs AVX2 (Intel Haswell /
# AMD Zen onwards) and cannot run on a CPU without it; aarch64 asks nothing
# special of the host. The engine also decides how Pocket's audio sounds, so
# changing it re-synthesizes Cedar's Pocket clips.
# CEDAR_POCKET_IMPL=python
# Which model speaks every Pocket voice, Cedar-side and container-side: the
# default "english" (the full model upstream serves), or one of the small and
# faster English models — english_18m_2026-10, english_7m_2026-10 or
# english_2m_2026-10, each also in a _refl variant. The small models are less
# faithful and stop sentences a touch sooner; on a slow CPU they are the way
# to keep preview latency down. Changing it also re-synthesizes.
# CEDAR_POCKET_MODEL=english
# The pinned upstream Pocket release both sides agree to. Bump only together
# with an engine change (docker-compose.pocket.yml's build args carry it).
# CEDAR_POCKET_VERSION=4.0.0
# Int8 inference (--quantize), off by default. Worth it on x86-64: upstream
# measures roughly 1.3x faster realtime with half the memory. Keep it off on
# ARM64 — the int8 backend (torchao) ships no ARM64 wheels, and the engine's
# fallback there runs slower than the float engine. Quantized weights draw
# different audio, so turning this on or off re-synthesizes Cedar's Pocket
# clips once.
# CEDAR_POCKET_QUANTIZE=0

# Optional Pocket TTS: a Hugging Face token for the gated model weights.
# Cloning a voice from your own recording needs them — accept the terms at
# huggingface.co/kyutai/pocket-tts, create a read token, and put it here.
# Without it Pocket runs the model that cannot clone, and only its built-in
# voices speak.
# HF_TOKEN=hf_...

# Optional direct HTTPS override: directory containing fullchain.pem and privkey.pem.
# CEDAR_TLS_CERT_DIR=/opt/cedar-certificates
